{"candidates": [{"conjecture": "Any finitely-presented algebraic specification that is confluent and terminating as a rewrite system admits a faithful interpretation into an executable Quotient Inductive Type (QIT), such that the generated eliminator provably enforces the equational theory. Thus, the QIT constructor signature is the canonical compilation target for confluent algebraic specifications, yielding code that holds its commitments by construction.", "probability": 0.09}, {"conjecture": "Compiling non-confluent algebraic specifications to QITs via naive constructor injection yields eliminator implementations that violate the specification's equations at runtime. Recovery requires synthesizing orientation via Knuth-Bendix completion prior to QIT generation; without this step, the executable term-rewriting system is fundamentally unsound with respect to its source.", "probability": 0.085}, {"conjecture": "The higher inductive-inductive type (HIIT) generated by a many-sorted algebraic specification has a canonical operational semantics given by pattern-matching on the constructors, and this semantics is complete (decides observational equivalence) if and only if the original specification admits a ground term model that is a free algebra modulo the equations.", "probability": 0.078}, {"conjecture": "A many-sorted algebraic specification can be soundly compiled to an executable term-rewriting system via an indexed QIT if and only if the specification's sort-respecting signature forms a generalized algebraic theory (GAT) in the sense of Cartmell, making GATs the exact boundary of the compilation method.", "probability": 0.072}, {"conjecture": "The computational content of a QIT's eliminator is exactly the most general unifier of the equational axioms, meaning that the best technique for turning an algebraic spec into code is to extract the eliminator's computational rules as a certified matching/unification algorithm and use it as the rewrite engine.", "probability": 0.068}, {"conjecture": "If two algebraic specifications present the same variety, their compiled QITs are judgmentally isomorphic in a Martin-Löf type theory with univalence, but the compiled term-rewriting systems differ in asymptotic complexity by a factor exponential in the difference between their constructor arities.", "probability": 0.063}, {"conjecture": "The quotient inductive type generated by an algebraic specification S is the initial algebra of the term monad of S, and its eliminator corresponds to the monad's fold. Therefore, the best compilation technique is to compute the monad's recursive bind as the operational semantics, yielding an executable rewrite system whose termination is equivalent to the monad being finitary.", "probability": 0.059}, {"conjecture": "An algebraic specification S can be compiled to a terminating executable term-rewriting system via QITs if and only if the equational theory of S is a locally finite variety, where local finiteness is equivalent to the QIT's induction principle being decidable.", "probability": 0.054}, {"conjecture": "Using a 1 HIT instead of a QIT to compile an algebraic spec to a rewrite system loses the ability to enforce equations between terms containing variables, limiting soundness to ground equations. Only QITs with proper higher constructors can faithfully represent schematic equations, making them strictly necessary for correct compilation of general algebraic theories.", "probability": 0.048}, {"conjecture": "The denotational complexity (number of constructors and equations) of the minimal QIT presenting a variety V is equal to the operational complexity (number of rewrite rules) of the minimal confluent terminating rewrite system for V plus the dimension of the space of critical pairs, establishing a precise tradeoff between specification size and rewrite efficiency.", "probability": 0.043}]}
